              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: what did tappakuṇḍā mean?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: ఏమో",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ఏమో (ēmō) — \"perhaps, who knows\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ఏమో (ēmō) is a shrug made into a word: perhaps, it may be so, who knows."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Look at the front of it and you will find the questioning ఏ- that already stands in ఏమిటి, \"what\". ఏమో (ēmō) is that question with the asking taken out and only the uncertainty left behind."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It also hangs on the end of a whole sentence, where English would need a separate clause: vastāḍēmō, \"he may come\". A language with a settled, unembarrassed word for not knowing is telling you something about its manners."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
